---
name: cluster
description: Notice the periods of heightened difficulty in a family's events and hold each as a hypothesis.
---
**Your task.** Read this family's events the way a clinician reads a family
history. Notice the periods when things were heightened for someone: shifts and
events piling up close together. A shift can be a fall as well as a rise:
functioning going down counts as much as a symptom or anxiety going up. Anxiety
is never written down for you; infer it from what piles up.

**Each period is a loose hypothesis.** Hold each one as a guess that some bigger
shift happened in the family around then: someone added, someone lost, someone's
position changed, even when nothing like that is recorded yet. A period needs no
visible opening event and no definite end; it ends where things look settled. A
family often has not connected its own troubles to what else was going on, so a
period drawn around one person's own problems is the usual place to start. You
do not have the whole story; do not hold a period tighter than the events allow.

**One reading per family.** The family is one emotional unit, so two periods
never cover the same days. Most events stay outside any period.

**What you are given.** First, the periods this diagram already has, each with
its id, name, reason and events; one marked "the person drew this period" is the
person's own. Every one of them is a hypothesis like yours: keep it, rename it,
reshape it, merge it or drop it as the events now read, the person's own
included. Return a period under the id of the one it grew out of. A period you
leave out is removed. Second, runs of events the app's rules found close in
time, each with the silence before it: a hint only, never a period by itself.
Third, every event.

**Name and reason.** Name each period for the hypothesis: what seems to have
shifted in the family, or the event that dominates it, and when, in words this
person would recognise; never its length, and never its years alone. The
`reason` is one sentence: which events pile up and what you suspect shifted.
When a period is not exactly as you were given it, `change` says in one
sentence, the way you would say it aloud about this person's story, what in the
events made you see it so.

Every period holds at least three events. A period covering the whole history is
the family's ordinary level, not a period. Never write an id that is not listed
below, and never add a detail that is not on the events you were given.

Return one entry per period: `id` when it grew out of a listed one, `eventIds`,
`name`, `reason`, and `change` when it is not exactly as given.

